<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sena Gallica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Senigallia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sena Gallica, modern Senigallia, is a town on the Adriatic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>. It was the main centre of the Gallic Senones, who settled the area after sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> around 390 BCE. After defeating the Senones, Rome founded a colony there in the 280s BCE, one of its first on the Adriatic, and the region became the ager Gallicus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sena-gallica.jpg" alt="Sena Gallica" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gaspa; MarkusMark; Luigi Chiesa; Steevie; derivative work by Number55 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Senigallia-collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Senigallia is a resort town and comune on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s Adriatic coast, in the province of Ancona in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>, about 30 kilometres north-west of Ancona. Its small port lies at the mouth of the River Misa. The town is one of the endpoints of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a>–Senigallia Line, an important dividing line, or isogloss, in the classification of the Romance languages. The name Sena is probably a corrupted form of Senones, and Gallica, meaning Gaulish, distinguished the place from Sena, the present Siena, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Gallic Senone tribe first settled the place in the 4th century BCE. In 284 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took over the area and the settlement and established the colony of Sena Gallica to control the region they called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ager-gallicus">Ager Gallicus</a>. In 207 BCE, in the prelude to the Battle of the Metaurus between Romans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>, Sena Gallica was the southernmost point of the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-barca">Hasdrubal Barca</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>Alaric ravaged the town as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> declined, and it was fortified when it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> laid it waste in the 8th century and the Saracens in the 9th. It was one of the five cities of the medieval Adriatic duchy of Pentapolis.</p><p>The diocese and bishopric were long established, and around the 13th century the town saw economic development, including the Magdalena Fair, visited by merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. In the 15th century the town was captured and recaptured many times in the Guelph and Ghibelline conflict. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismondo-pandolfo-malatesta">Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta</a> of Rimini fortified it in 1450–1455, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> later assigned the lordship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-rovere">Della Rovere</a> family. In 1503 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> ordered a series of executions there as revenge for the Magione conspiracy, in which princes who had been his military allies plotted to remove him.</p><p>In 1516 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> transferred the lordship to Lorenzo II de' Medici, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-rovere">Della Rovere</a> took it again, until in 1624 the town was annexed to the Papal States' legation of Urbino. Giovanni Maria Mastai Ferretti, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>, was born there in 1792.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the town and its port were devastated by shelling from Austro-Hungarian naval units led by the battleship SMS Zrínyi. A strong earthquake damaged it in 1930, and it suffered again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, so that much of the present city is modern. Its sights include the Rocca Roveresca, a castle of Gothic origins restored by Baccio Pontelli in 1492, the 17th-century Palazzo Comunale and the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan">Santa Maria delle Grazie</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Senigallia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At <b>Senigallia</b> that month he trapped and killed rebellious captains.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-romagna">Duchy of Romagna</a></li><li><blockquote>Livy and Polybius record that part of the people crossed the Alps around 400 BCE; led by Brennus, they defeated the Romans at the Allia and sacked Rome, then settled on the Adriatic coast around <b>Sena Gallica</b> (Senigallia).</blockquote><a href="/state/senones">Senones</a></li><li><blockquote>After they defeated a Roman army at Arretium in 284 BCE, Rome invaded their land in 283 BCE, killing or expelling the Senones, and founded the colony of <b>Sena Gallica</b> (Senigallia) there.</blockquote><a href="/state/senones-400">Senones</a></li></ul></section>
